      "weight_variable_name": null,
      "weight_fields_interpretation": "The study uses PISA 2018 data for 37 OECD countries and constructs a country-level classification target based on whether each country’s standardized mean scores exceed OECD-average thresholds (487 reading, 489 mathematics, 489 science). The manuscript reports that there was no missing data and describes extensive preprocessing (categorizing continuous variables, removing insignificant variables, and standardizing variable names). However, it does not report whether PISA sampling weights or replicate weights were applied, nor does it mention any weight variable (e.g., W_FSTUWT) or complex survey design adjustments. Given the use of Random Forest with the Boruta feature-selection wrapper, the analysis appears to be conducted without explicit survey-weight incorporation; the paper does not provide enough methodological detail to confirm whether weights were used."
    },
    "plausible_values_handling": "average_pv",
    "missing_data_handling": "not_reported",
    "handling_not_reported_explanation": "The paper states that there was no missing data in the dataset, but it does not describe any explicit missing-data procedure (e.g., deletion or imputation) beyond that statement. Additionally, the dependent variables are country-level standardized PISA scores derived from PISA achievement domains; the manuscript does not document how PISA plausible values (PV-based achievement scores) were aggregated into the standardized scores used for modeling. This creates a reporting gap regarding PV handling and the exact treatment of missingness/aggregation steps.",
