    "outcome_summary": "The study applies Random Forest (via R and the Boruta feature-selection wrapper) to identify influential variables and to build predictive models for whether OECD countries are high-performing versus low-performing in PISA 2018 reading, mathematics, and science. Using OECD-average thresholds (487 reading, 489 math, 489 science), the authors report that the most important variables across domains include ICT access (ICTRES), weekly instructional time (CPERWEEK), socioeconomic/cultural status (ESCS), metacognitive awareness (METASPAM), and parental occupational status (HISEI). Domain-specific additional important variables are CPERFORLAN for reading, COMPETE for mathematics and science, and PISADIFF and JOYREAD for science. The paper provides linear-form prediction equations (Model 1 reading, Model 2 math, Model 3 science) and states that countries predicted above model cutoffs (reading > 3.219, math > 2.396, science > 2.732) can be successful, enabling an “early warning system” for policy makers. The manuscript does not report standard ML performance metrics (e.g., accuracy/AUC/RMSE) for the predictive models, and it cautions that Random Forest variable importance does not imply causality.",
